A||||3||Posthumane||Quad 4 swap question||||1||
Z||000000||Posthumane||12-24-2001||01:22 AM||||I am almost certain now that I want to do a quad 4 swap. My question is about the kits. As in do I need one? what kits are out there? which ones are better than the others? I plan on doing the work myself. How hard is the rewiring for the swap on an 84 (SE) compared to an 85 coupe? Also what tranny could I get if I got the motor out of an automatic car (not likely, but if I don't find a good standard)? <BR>Sum: Should I bother with a kit, or should I try to figure it out on my own? I have some mechanical experience, but nothing this big. What even comes with the kits?<P>Sorry for all the questions. would really appreciate some opinions/info. Thanx.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Smoooooth GT||12-24-2001||01:54 PM||||You sure have some good ?'s...   good luck!!  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smooooothgtsig_9.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BABIE_BUN_SHAKERS.gif">                       <BR>      <P><A HREF="http://www.thefibersmith.com" TARGET=_blank>www.thefibersmith.com</A>   <BR>Technical  Support (903) 825-7744 Steve<BR>"Smoooooth GT"<BR>Shaved door handles, sidetrim, exhaust outlet's, etc.... smooooth!!!||||reg||1
Z||000002||MoneypitGT||12-24-2001||06:14 PM||||Well lets put it this way, a good kit for example from ACE is around $1,800, now that is just the kit, then you need a motor and tranny, (the Q4 has its own bellhousing) a decent HO (if it is not an HO don't waste your time, also 5pd only)is going to be about $1,500 if you can find one. I personally didn't use a kit(then again I'm not done yet either,) but if you have some welding/fabrication skills you can make your own, (you need two make two motor mounts, the tranny mounts in almost an identical way as the fiero's)or even have a shop do it. That is entirely up to you and what you think you can do, also the wiring is not cool if you don't know much about car electrical systems. That is my 2 cents, I would also see what Phreder has to say, he has done I think 2 or them. <BR> Chris<P>------------------<BR>  <B>Check out my site at:</B>   <A HREF="http://www.customdynamix.com" TARGET=_blank>www.customdynamix.com</A> <BR>Turbo Q4 GT Project||||reg||6
Z||000003||pherder||12-24-2001||07:02 PM||||Personal opinion is to stay away from the ACE kit ... they bolt the torque arm to the aluminum head ... not my idea of a good practice.<P>As MoneypitGT stated, the HO is the best engine and the years to look for is 90-92.  There are HOs available in 89 and 93-94 but there are some issues with those although if you can't find a 90-92 but can find one of the other years ... go for it.<P>Get as much as you can from the donor car.  Whatever you don't get, you will need and have to buy later.  If you can get the ENTIRE front compartment (or front end) of the donor car, you will be better off.  Buying the parts later is very expensive ... experienced that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>As MoneypitGT stated, the HOs ONLY came with a 5 speed.  Since they were designed for FWD, you might find that you will have to come up with custom brackets to mount the front of the transaxle.  Also there are three different clutch hydraulics ... slave like the Fiero's that pushes an external arm, a slave that pushes on an internal arm and a hydraulic actuated throwout bearing.  The first two are the easiest to adapt to the Fiero, the last requires an aftermarket clutch master cylinder.<P>I used the stock transaxle rubber mounts modified to put the engine/transaxle over ~ 1/2" to the left like Archie does with his V8 swap.  This allows clearance for the torque arm that holds the engine lid to clear the Q4's cam chain tower.  I used one engine mount in front (basically a "L" bracket like is used on the 85-87 V6 engines) to two poly biscuits to the stock front cradle engine mount.<P>Wiring is a pain in the ~!$#~!.  I had several problems ... one I was trying to use my ACE install manual's wiring harness diagrams and they only confused me.  I ended up just using the factory wiring diagrams and making sure EACH and EVERY wire from the computer went where it needed to be, to include the wires that the Fiero needed.  I did my two Q4 swaps with an 86 and an 88 so I don't have any experience modifying an 84.  Get a Helms Factory Service manual for both the car that the engine came from and the Fiero that you are installing it into.<P>The signal coming out of the VSS of the Q4 transaxle will NOT run the Fiero's speedo correctly.  Joe Wynmann wrote an article for Fiero Owner (FOCOA)  that is a signal modifier, a very simple circuit that takes the output from the Q4's ECM and mods it so that the Fiero's speedo will work and display correctly.<P>I used an 87-88 throttle cable with a bracket bolted/welded to the Q4's "box" to take up the ~ 1" extra length.  The "box" is two pulleys that attaches the throttle cable, the cable from the throttle body and the cable from the cruise control (and the TV cable from the automatic which isn't used here.)<P>The 88 L4 left engine coolant hose fits perfectly.  The right side needs to be modified.  I ran the metal coolant line from the water pump under the engine and had it come up under the A/C compressor.  From there I used one of the bend to fit flex coolant pipes (bendable aluminum, I think) to attach to the stock coolant line.<P>I used the stock L4 heater hose (88) that goes to the rear of the engine bay and attached it with a "T" to the thermostat and a MOROSO coolant tank which I used as the high fill point.  It was mounted ~ where the Fiero's engine torque bracket was (I removed it since it wasn't going to be used.)<P>The Fiero's axles will fit without mods.<P>Exhaust manifold is at the rear of the car so it doesn't allow much room for the exhaust system.  Saying that I was able to have a cat. convertor and a stock replacement muffler system (Ocelot from The Fiero Store) installed in the rear.  It is tight and makes a LOT of heat!  I had it coated to try to keep the temperatures down but there is still A LOT of heat there.<P>My brain is going numb so I will stop now.  When you have more questions, I will try to answer them.<P>BTW, I will never do another.<P>Paul||||reg||1
